# Independently authored SHOW catch-all (EmptyShow
# fallback) smoke for the GPL MTR-format runner. NOT
# copied or adapted from MySQL's mysql-test/.
#
# Existing coverage (do NOT duplicate):
#   - show_basic_extra covers SHOW DATABASES, SHOW
#     TABLES, SHOW FULL TABLES, SHOW COLUMNS, SHOW
#     CREATE TABLE, SHOW INDEX, SHOW TABLES LIKE.
#   - show_empty_stubs_basic_extra covers SHOW ERRORS,
#     SHOW CHARSET, SHOW PRIVILEGES.
#   - charset_collation_basic_extra covers SHOW
#     COLLATION (specifically).
#   - This slice covers the REMAINING SHOW variants
#     that the mysqlwire shim catch-alls to EmptyShow
#     at shim.rs:2023.
#
# This slice pins:
#   - SHOW EVENTS — silent empty (zeta has no scheduler;
#     PR #373 pinned the CREATE/ALTER/DROP EVENT 1064
#     reject — the SHOW path is the asymmetric silent
#     companion).
#   - SHOW MASTER STATUS — silent empty (no MySQL
#     binlog; zeta uses log-store + pgoutput).
#   - SHOW SLAVE STATUS — silent empty.
#   - SHOW BINARY LOGS — silent empty.
#   - SHOW FUNCTION STATUS — silent empty.
#   - SHOW PROCEDURE STATUS — silent empty.
#   - SHOW TRIGGERS — silent empty (zeta supports
#     CREATE TRIGGER per CLAUDE.md, but the SHOW path
#     doesn't actually enumerate them).
#   - SHOW PLUGINS — silent empty (no plugin
#     architecture).
#   - SHOW KEYS FROM <table> alias for SHOW INDEX —
#     silent empty when the table doesn't exist (no
#     1146 error since the catch-all swallows).
#   - SHOW FIELDS FROM <table> alias for SHOW COLUMNS —
#     same silent empty when table doesn't exist.
#
# Big-picture finding (UNIVERSAL SHOW CATCH-ALL):
#
#   The mysqlwire shim at shim.rs:1904-2023 intercepts
#   ANY query starting with "show " and uses substring
#   matching to dispatch. If NO specific handler matches
#   (line 2023: `return Some(MySqlResult::EmptyShow)`),
#   the query SILENTLY returns zero rows.
#
#   This means MANY made-up SHOW variants ALSO silently
#   succeed. Apps trying `SHOW XYZZY` or `SHOW
#   nonsense_command` will see "0 rows returned" rather
#   than the MySQL 1064 parse error they expect. This
#   is a significant DIVERGENCE from PG-style errors-on-
#   unknown and from MySQL-style 1064-on-unknown.
#
#   STRIKING — even garbage SHOW commands SUCCEED. Apps
#   doing `SHOW FAKE_VARIABLE` will see empty results,
#   thinking the variable doesn't exist. They should
#   probe via the explicit `SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'X'`
#   form (or @@-syntax) to distinguish "no such variable"
#   from "variable exists but unset".
#
#   STRIKING — SHOW KEYS / SHOW FIELDS on a nonexistent
#   table also returns EmptyShow silently. Real MySQL
#   would error 1146. Apps probing for table existence
#   via SHOW KEYS / SHOW FIELDS should switch to
#   `information_schema.tables` instead.
#
#   For features that DO exist in zeta but lack SHOW
#   support (TRIGGERS), the catch-all silently produces
#   empty results — an asymmetric gap (CREATE works but
#   SHOW doesn't enumerate). Apps relying on
#   `SHOW TRIGGERS` for introspection should use
#   `pg_catalog.pg_trigger` or `information_schema.
#   triggers` instead.
#
# Adaptation notes:
#   # zeta-adapt: The mysqlwire shim's SHOW catch-all
#     returns empty rows for ANY unrecognized SHOW
#     variant — NO 1064 error like real MySQL. Apps
#     can't probe for "does this SHOW form exist".
#   # zeta-adapt: SHOW TRIGGERS / SHOW EVENTS /
#     SHOW PLUGINS / SHOW MASTER STATUS / SHOW BINARY
#     LOGS / etc all return empty. Use information_
#     schema or pg_catalog for real introspection.
#   # zeta-adapt: SHOW KEYS / SHOW FIELDS on nonexistent
#     table → empty (no 1146). Use INFORMATION_SCHEMA
#     for existence probes.

--echo === SHOW EVENTS — silent empty (CREATE/ALTER/DROP EVENT errors 1064 per PR #373) ===

SHOW EVENTS;

--echo === SHOW MASTER STATUS / SHOW SLAVE STATUS / SHOW BINARY LOGS — silent empty ===

SHOW MASTER STATUS;
SHOW SLAVE STATUS;
SHOW BINARY LOGS;

--echo === SHOW FUNCTION STATUS / SHOW PROCEDURE STATUS — silent empty ===

SHOW FUNCTION STATUS;
SHOW PROCEDURE STATUS;

--echo === SHOW TRIGGERS — silent empty even though CREATE TRIGGER works ===

# Asymmetric gap: zeta supports CREATE TRIGGER but
# SHOW TRIGGERS doesn't enumerate them.
SHOW TRIGGERS;

--echo === SHOW PLUGINS — silent empty (no plugin architecture) ===

SHOW PLUGINS;

--echo === STRIKING: garbage SHOW also returns empty (no 1064 error) ===

# Real MySQL would error on these unknown SHOW forms.
# zeta's catch-all swallows them silently.
SHOW MADE_UP_THING;
SHOW XYZZY;
SHOW SOMETHING_NONSENSE;

--echo === SHOW KEYS / SHOW FIELDS on nonexistent table — empty (no 1146) ===

# Real MySQL: ERROR 1146 'no such table'. zeta: silent empty.
SHOW KEYS FROM definitely_does_not_exist;
SHOW FIELDS FROM also_does_not_exist;

# NOTE: The asymmetric gap (CREATE TRIGGER works but
# SHOW TRIGGERS returns empty) can't easily be pinned
# in MTR-format because the trigger-function body uses
# `$$` dollar-quotes containing semicolons, which the
# MTR runner splits on. Trigger creation/enumeration is
# covered in other tests; the SHOW TRIGGERS empty path
# is sufficiently pinned by the standalone `SHOW
# TRIGGERS;` row above.
